{"task_id": "Mbpp/11", "solution": "def remove_Occ(s: str, char: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Removes the first and last occurrence of a given character from the string.\"\"\"\n    first_index = s.find(char)\n    if first_index == -1:\n        return s  # Character not found\n\n    # Remove the first occurrence\n    temp_s = s[:first_index] + s[first_index + 1:]\n\n    # Find the last occurrence in the original string, adjust index for temp_s\n    last_index_original = s.rfind(char)\n\n    if last_index_original == first_index:\n        return temp_s # Only one occurrence, which has already been removed\n\n    # If the last_index_original is after first_index, it means we removed an earlier char,\n    # so the effective index in temp_s is last_index_original - 1\n    # If the last_index_original is before first_index (not possible in this logic flow if first_index is the true first),\n    # or if first_index was the only occurrence.\n    # The simpler way is to re-find in the temp_s.\n    \n    # We need to remove the last occurrence from the *original* string's perspective\n    # Then reconstruct.\n    # Simpler: find first, find last. If same, remove one. If different, remove both.\n\n    # Re-evaluate with two separate removal points\n    first_idx = s.find(char)\n    last_idx = s.rfind(char)\n\n    if first_idx == -1:\n        return s\n\n    if first_idx == last_idx:\n        # Only one occurrence\n        return s[:first_idx] + s[first_idx+1:]\n    else:\n        # Multiple occurrences\n        # Remove last occurrence first to avoid index shifting issues for the first one\n        s_without_last = s[:last_idx] + s[last_idx+1:]\n        # Find the first occurrence again in the string without its original last\n        # (or just use the original first_idx if it's still valid)\n        return s_without_last[:first_idx] + s_without_last[first_idx+1:]"}
